WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.800
I am Alena Pance and I am a scientist at the Sanger Institute.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:21.600
I specialise in cell and molecular biology
and use these skills to study Malaria.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:29.040
In simple terms Malaria is a debilitating disease that affects the tropical world and beyond.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.880
It is caused by a single cell parasite transmitted from person to person by mosquitoes.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:43.280
The Malaria parasite has a complex life cycle and in the human host goes through several stages.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.600
In the lab I concentrate on the blood life cycle.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:56.800
Malaria parasites are infectious organisms so in order to work with them we need a special lab.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.120
These are CL3 or Containment Level 3 laboratories.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:11.720
The purpose of the CL3 is to protect the workers outside as well as those inside working with the infectious organism.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.800
For this we need protective clothing, 
in the form of a lab coat and gloves.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:28.240
We culture the parasite in plastic flasks and petri dishes, using blood from the NHS blood and transfusion centre.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.280
Malaria parasites need special conditions to grow in the lab;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:43.880
a specific mix of gas, a 37 degree environment - to mimic the body temperature - and a fresh supply of blood.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:52.960
In the CL3 we work in hoods, this serves to protect the worker from the parasite and also

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.640
to protect the cultures from any contaminating agents that might be in the environment.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.320
In the hood we have bleach containers to dispose of anything that has been in contact with the parasite.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:15.640
We take a sample of the culture, we put it on a slide and smear it, let it dry,

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:22.000
then the slide needs to be fixed and stained with Giemsa,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.680
which is a blue colourant that will allow us to see the red blood cells and the parasites on a microscope.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:39.200
On the microscope, what we look for is the stages of the parasite that are present in the culture,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:51.000
so we see the red blood cells in light blue and within the red blood cells we can see darker purplish forms of the parasite.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:59.560
The very small ones are Rings that will then grow into bigger parasites called Trophozoites

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.680
and these will start multiplying and forming a Shizont.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:14.600
This is the mature form of the parasite that will burst out of the red blood cell and invade new cells.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:21.240
We genetically modify the parasite 
to knock out or modify certain genes

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.640
in order to assess whether these are involved in the disease.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:34.760
We also introduce fluorescent proteins into the parasite to be able to follow it throughout the infection

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:40.240
and assess its ability to invade particular red blood cells.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:49.560
I was always fascinated by the natural world and I always wanted to understand how things work.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:58.680
To be able to see the cells and the parasites, and work on their interactions, that is very exciting.

